  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\n08Z water vapor imagery had a closed upper low along the NV/OR state\nline with southwest flow over the central plains. Surface obs placed\na low pressure center over the CO high plains with weak high \npressure over the middle MS river valley. This has favored low level \nsoutherly return flow to persist. \n\nFor today and tonight, the concerns are wind speeds and fire weather \npotential. Surface low pressure is progged to remain to the west and \nnorth of the forecast area, but it doesn't really deepen a lot \nthrough the day. This is expected to keep a tight pressure gradient \nacross the forecast area. However the magnitude of the gradient is \njust a little weaker than I would like to see it for a wind \nadvisory. Models show about a 8MB difference from north central KS \nto east central KS, and typically winds strong enough for a wind \nadvisory are caused with gradients of 10 or more millibars across \nthe forecast area. Additionally the objective MOS guidance for CNK \nkeeps the magnitude of the wind just below advisory criteria. It is \ngoing to be windy with gusts around 35 MPH, but think conditions are \ntoo marginal for an advisory. The day shift can keep an eye on this \nthrough the morning. The gusty winds bring up the potential for fire \nweather concerns, especially after we've had a couple hard freezes \nand fuels are beginning to cure. But the southerly winds are \nforecast to keep dewpoint temps in the 50 to 55 degree range through \nthe afternoon. Forecast soundings maintain an inversion around 850MB \nwhich prevents mixing up to where the dryer air is. Because of this \nminimum RH values should hold around 50 percent and mitigate \nsomewhat the fire weather concerns for today. Think with enough \ninsolation and persistent southerly winds, temps will push back to \nwhere they were yesterday and perhaps a degree or so warmer. \n\nA disturbance and frontal boundary are forecast to push west to east \nacross the forecast area Saturday afternoon and evening. Lift from \nthe front and forcing from an upper trough is expected to create \nshowers and storms by the late afternoon Saturday as most guidance \nincluding all the ensemble data show categorical POPs. Severe \nweather chances will be driven by whether enough destabilization can \noccur ahead of the front. Forecast sounding show good bulk shear \nvalues for organized updrafts. However dewpoints don't really shoot \nup ahead of the front and mid level lapse rates remain around 7 \nC/km. Initially there may be some mid level instability around 300 \nJ/kg. But forecast soundings quickly shallow up lapse rates to near \nthe moist adiabat thanks to wetbulb cooling. So am not overly \nconcerned with severe weather at this point. \n\nMuch colder air arrives for Sunday as a Canadian surface high builds \ninto the central plains. Ensembles show reasonable spread in temps \nfor Sunday through Thursday. So the NBM should be a reasonable first \nguess on temps with the surface ridge making it feel a lot more like \nwinter with lows in the 20s and highs in the 40s. An upper trough is \nstill progged to move through the central plains Tuesday and Tuesday \nnight. However the lack of moisture within the surface ridge and \nlittle sign of moisture advection this far north will preclude \nchances for precip.",
